{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Schwarz","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Analytic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.norm_le_of_re_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.norm_le_of_re_le","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BorelCaratheodory.lean#L82-L153","doc":"**Borel-Carathéodory inequality.** A one-sided bound on the real part of a\nholomorphic `f` with `f 0 = 0` yields a two-sided bound on its modulus strictly\ninside the disc.\n\nNo `0 ≤ r` hypothesis is needed: for `r < 0` the hypothesis `‖z‖ ≤ r` is\nunsatisfiable, and the proof never case-splits on the sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.two_mul_sub_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.two_mul_sub_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BorelCaratheodory.lean#L73-L80","doc":"`2M - w ≠ 0` whenever `Re w ≤ M` and `0 < M`: its real part is at least `M`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.norm_le_norm_two_mul_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BorelCaratheodory.norm_le_norm_two_mul_sub","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BorelCaratheodory.lean#L57-L71","doc":"If `Re w ≤ M` and `0 < M` then `‖w‖ ≤ ‖2M - w‖`.\n\nThis is the engine: it converts a ONE-SIDED bound on the real part into a\nTWO-SIDED bound on the modulus of the Möbius transform. Geometrically, the\nhalf-plane `Re w ≤ M` is exactly the set of points at least as close to `0` as\nto `2M`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}